1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,880
Then Ben turned up and blew the mixer up, I was playing after him, so he blew the headphone

2
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:17,039
jack up so I had to DJ for an hour on vinyl with no headphones, so I didn't care because

3
00:00:17,039 --> 00:00:22,600
I got to meet Ben and it was, you know, we'd felt that like oh my god, we've not just

4
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:28,280
put a party on with friends, we've put a party on and we've booked, you know,

5
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:30,280
the guy at the time, because obviously...

6
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:52,159
Welcome to the very first episode of the Valkyrie Podcast.

7
00:00:52,159 --> 00:00:55,920
I'm your host, Tom aka Tangents from Valkyrie.

8
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:01,000
The aim with these podcasts is to create a place for long-form interesting conversations

9
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,200
with our artists and people that we care about and I'm really excited to kick things

10
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:11,240
off today with the mighty Killbox aka EdRussianAudio.

11
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:15,799
Now at Valkyrie, we're celebrating Killbox's comeback EP, which has just come out as

12
00:01:15,799 --> 00:01:20,240
the debut release on our label, Killer of Killers, so I encourage you to all

13
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,780
check that out, it's a wicked EP, it's amazing, go listen to it.

14
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:28,939
And today, we're not just going to get into aspects of the EP, but we're going to go

15
00:01:28,939 --> 00:01:35,780
way deeper into their artist journey, origins of the scene, changing face of the scene,

16
00:01:35,780 --> 00:01:40,420
technology, aliens, there's all sorts of stuff in here, so yeah, I really enjoyed

17
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:45,460
having this conversation with the Killbox guys, so I hope you enjoy it too.

18
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:50,219
So to kick things off, we're going to dive straight into the middle of the conversation

19
00:01:50,219 --> 00:01:56,539
where we're discussing the prospect of EdRush getting on the mic during shows.

20
00:01:56,539 --> 00:02:01,140
I did it on the Australian tour, I did it in November, there was a couple of daytime raves

21
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:06,019
and Wilkinson was on before me and he was like, every couple of tunes he was going for it

22
00:02:06,019 --> 00:02:12,060
and I felt like that was adding on to the energy, so I felt like it was necessary

23
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:16,340
and I wasn't too smashed, I could string a sentence together still, so I'm like,

24
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:19,259
let's have a little go, so that wasn't too bad.

25
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:24,859
Just here and there randomly, like Japan, I've done it a couple of times and things like that.

26
00:02:24,859 --> 00:02:29,579
I think there's a time and a place, there's a time where it's essential

27
00:02:29,579 --> 00:02:34,419
and you've really just got to dig deep and just do it, even though it's uncomfortable, it's horrible.

28
00:02:34,419 --> 00:02:37,819
You hopped on the mic for a little speech at the end of Neuroheads last year

29
00:02:37,819 --> 00:02:39,979
and that felt right.

30
00:02:39,979 --> 00:02:45,419
Yeah, exactly, it's organic and it feels like it's going to add on in a good way.

31
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:49,700
It felt very community vibe there, so it felt like the right thing to do.

32
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:53,580
Do you reckon different audiences expect different things with the mics and stuff?

33
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:56,620
I don't know, it seems like the American audiences, I've been hearing things

34
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:59,580
with the great find that certain DJs are being encouraged to do this.

35
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:05,820
I think in the States especially, if there's no MC, I think a lot of the ravers

36
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:12,180
and the festival crowd kind of expect a bit of communication, it's nice to hear a human voice.

37
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:14,460
You know what, that's interesting, I hadn't thought about it that way actually

38
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:19,540
because I guess often times we kind of take the MC host duties for granted sometimes.

39
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:20,620
Yes, yeah you do.

40
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:27,580
And you know what, I never thought about it that way, not having a host for an EDM artist or something.

41
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:30,460
And it can become quite stilted, can't it? You go on.

42
00:03:30,460 --> 00:03:35,500
Well I mean, it was like the fundamental parts of Jungle and Drummer Base were to have,

43
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:40,219
you had a host, you had an MC, when I first got into it, that was part of it.

44
00:03:40,259 --> 00:03:43,900
It was only when you started really going to sort of Europe,

45
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:49,259
where they didn't perhaps have that connection with Darb and Reggae and they were doing Drummer Base,

46
00:03:49,259 --> 00:03:56,340
they would find it like, why have you got someone talking, it's annoying, it's detracting from the tracks.

47
00:03:56,340 --> 00:04:02,340
So yeah, I think that influence is better now in Europe where they kind of understand

48
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:05,180
that Drummer Base has an MC and you know, they're a little bit,

49
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:08,740
but you still go to places don't you, where they're just, they're not interested,

50
00:04:08,740 --> 00:04:12,659
they just want to hear you play music, which is great, because that's what I'm there to do.

51
00:04:12,659 --> 00:04:16,740
So I can quite easily not talk for two hours and just play tunes.

52
00:04:16,740 --> 00:04:20,180
Yeah, for sure. But like, it's just interesting to see the differences, isn't it?

53
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:25,540
Yeah, and the amount of comments you see online of like, I wish there was no MC on this set.

54
00:04:25,540 --> 00:04:27,900
But it's different across the world.

55
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:34,019
You might get people in certain parts of the world saying that and other people enjoy it and think it adds on in a positive way.

56
00:04:34,019 --> 00:04:37,019
But it definitely comes from like Sound Clash culture.

57
00:04:37,019 --> 00:04:37,620
Yeah.

58
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:41,659
From sort of, you know, hollering, shouting for the rewind and stuff like that.

59
00:04:41,659 --> 00:04:43,299
That's kind of the roots of it all.

60
00:04:43,299 --> 00:04:43,699
Yeah.

61
00:04:43,699 --> 00:04:49,459
And some parts of the world didn't really have that as a part of growing up for them.

62
00:04:49,459 --> 00:04:51,899
Whereas we did, it's part of British culture.

63
00:04:51,899 --> 00:04:54,459
Yeah, that's one of the things I love about this scene.

64
00:04:54,459 --> 00:04:59,180
Like everyone has their own route in and like the Valkyrie crew are quite an international crew,

65
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:03,659
you know, like Annie's come from Singapore, from the Asian side of things.

66
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:07,460
I learned from Ukraine, like it's all different.

67
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:13,740
Everyone's got their own kind of story and stuff that I might kind of expect or take for granted growing up in the UK,

68
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:17,540
like UK things, like they've had different experiences and they see it with new eyes.

69
00:05:17,540 --> 00:05:18,780
I think that's so interesting.

70
00:05:18,780 --> 00:05:19,900
Yeah, absolutely.

71
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:20,420
Yeah.

72
00:05:20,420 --> 00:05:21,020
Yeah.

73
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:23,860
Talking about kind of like the scene stuff, one of the things I wanted to talk to you guys about,

74
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:26,820
like you connected from like the sort of free party scene, right?

75
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:33,420
Like back in the day, like tell us a little bit about that, because that's a whole chapter that like my generation and others,

76
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:35,259
we weren't able to participate in that.

77
00:05:35,259 --> 00:05:35,980
Yeah.

78
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:40,740
I think especially as like nostalgia is always super powerful, you see it in the branding,

79
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:43,900
you see it like the traces of it in the culture.

80
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:46,340
But like was one like a spiral tribe?

81
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:52,939
Yeah, it's kind of before it was hit the clubs, before it was commercial in any way.

82
00:05:52,939 --> 00:05:53,460
Yeah.

83
00:05:53,460 --> 00:06:00,819
Before you found it on in any kind of major clubs in any city, there was a big movement within the music

84
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:03,379
through pirate radio stations and stuff like that.

85
00:06:03,379 --> 00:06:06,019
I worked on three or four pirate radio stations.

86
00:06:06,019 --> 00:06:07,740
What were you doing there?

87
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:11,819
DJing, Don FM, Flex FM.

88
00:06:11,819 --> 00:06:18,180
And we would find someone would allow us to use their apartment or their flat for the day.

89
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:21,779
And you'd set the rig up on that is normally on a council estate on a high rise.

90
00:06:21,779 --> 00:06:24,219
So you could put the aerial right up to reach maximum.

91
00:06:24,219 --> 00:06:25,539
Like Corrupt FM.

92
00:06:25,539 --> 00:06:26,420
Exactly.

93
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:27,180
Exactly that.

94
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:28,100
It was basically that.

95
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:30,300
And you just move from flat to flat.

96
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:32,660
And if you got caught, they'd confiscate all the gear.

97
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:36,220
You'd lose all your tunes, all your dub plates, which was terrifying,

98
00:06:36,220 --> 00:06:39,180
because I'd go there with like a grand's worth of dub plates.

99
00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:40,980
And if they were gone, that was it.

100
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:41,620
Like no.

101
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:44,259
Yeah, you can't just whack out another USB and you're good to go.

102
00:06:44,259 --> 00:06:45,980
Yeah, exactly.

103
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:55,939
And the pirate radio stations would kind of work in conjunction with a lot of the spiral tribe guys as far as sharing information.

104
00:06:55,939 --> 00:06:58,100
So it'd be like the parties in Oxford.

105
00:06:58,100 --> 00:06:59,860
We don't quite know where it is yet.

106
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:03,460
But we're meeting at this petrol station at 11 p.m.

107
00:07:03,460 --> 00:07:05,660
on junction blah, blah of the M25.

108
00:07:05,660 --> 00:07:12,100
That kind of information like arms race, just keeping one step ahead of all the authorities or people you don't want to show up.

109
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:14,420
200 cars would show up at this petrol station.

110
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:17,819
It would be like Chinese whispers like, oh, it's at so-and-so, it's at so-and-so.

111
00:07:17,819 --> 00:07:22,180
And then this big convoy would drive off to this poor farmer's field.

112
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:25,180
They just got jacked for the night.

113
00:07:25,220 --> 00:07:32,019
See, Ben lived in London, so he sort of had access to those sort of parties where I lived sort of out in the countryside and out on a stick.

114
00:07:32,019 --> 00:07:36,819
So, yeah, I would just I wouldn't be able to travel to pirate to do pirate radio station,

115
00:07:36,819 --> 00:07:43,579
but I would have a hanger outside the window trying to pick up, you know, reception and record tapes of it.

116
00:07:43,579 --> 00:07:51,460
And then, yeah, like my way in was doing free parties with with friends, just setting up in disused buildings,

117
00:07:51,460 --> 00:07:54,300
sitting a rig up and just hoping the police don't turn up.

118
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:56,740
Many times the police did turn up and shut everything down.

119
00:07:56,740 --> 00:08:00,900
But, yeah, I think that's that energy that kind of do it yourself.

120
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:06,420
Energy has always been in dance music and EDM and especially from the UK.

121
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:10,580
So, yeah, I think we both just we both came in that way, but just different angles.

122
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:12,420
Again, we're talking about angles of things.

123
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:17,220
Yeah, yeah, I definitely see that like creativity, ingenuity and like that rebelliousness.

124
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:20,180
Like that is right in the DNA of this music.

125
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:21,180
It's like punk, isn't it?

126
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:22,940
Yeah, it's that it's that rebellion.

127
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:24,060
We go against the system.

128
00:08:24,060 --> 00:08:26,660
We do what we want when we want to do mentality.

129
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:35,700
And we just instead of being like with a band, it's now with, you know, with big sound systems and decks and records and stuff.

130
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:36,660
So, yeah.

131
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:42,820
And you feel like you're part of really something special because it's always like a small community that start off with those movements.

132
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:49,780
Yeah. And you feel like you're part of this kind of exclusive thing and that not most people don't know about it.

133
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:57,860
And that's that's really cool because you get that sense of community and, like, you know, connection with other people with similar interests and stuff.

134
00:08:57,860 --> 00:09:00,900
Yeah. And I heard someone else talk about it, like another DJ in the scene talk about it.

135
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:06,220
Like it was like a secret, like exclusive club, but the only requirement is like you just have to show up and get involved.

136
00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:10,660
Yeah. Once you step through that door, like, yeah, you're in your world.

137
00:09:10,699 --> 00:09:16,699
We were talking about yesterday in another interview about Valkyrie and what you guys are trying to do with this community.

138
00:09:16,699 --> 00:09:21,500
And it really feels that you're bringing that sense of community back or trying to that.

139
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:29,659
I feel that we've kind of lost, really, where everything's a bit faceless and, you know, impersonal and stuff.

140
00:09:29,659 --> 00:09:37,220
It feels like you're trying to bring that back, which is which is a lovely thing, because it's how I it was one of the most it was one of the attractive parts of

141
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:45,220
drum and bass or electronic music at that point was that sense of community wasn't just I love the music and what what it said to me and how it touched my soul.

142
00:09:45,220 --> 00:09:51,139
It was also that feeling of like I'm with this with brothers and sisters who feel exactly the same as well as I do.

143
00:09:51,139 --> 00:09:54,779
And it's feel as strongly as I do about this thing.

144
00:09:54,779 --> 00:09:59,060
So we're all like minded people. So you just naturally feel comfortable.

145
00:09:59,060 --> 00:10:03,779
Any ideas that you had didn't feel silly to say because everyone would get where you're coming from.

146
00:10:03,779 --> 00:10:05,659
Do you know what I mean? 100 percent like that.

147
00:10:05,659 --> 00:10:13,939
That resonates super strongly with me because my experience moving to London like on my own and then I started going out to events on my own because my friends quickly,

148
00:10:13,939 --> 00:10:19,899
like they ran out of steam and didn't have the same level of obsession for going to everything that I could.

149
00:10:19,899 --> 00:10:24,139
You know, that was like now that I had this new world that was open to me.

150
00:10:24,139 --> 00:10:27,379
And you just naturally like you form these connections with people.

151
00:10:27,379 --> 00:10:32,339
And it's been interesting for me to see, like some of my peers, like I'm I'm in my early 30s now.

152
00:10:32,899 --> 00:10:35,340
Some of them feel a bit isolated in London sometimes.

153
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:39,379
You know, like it can be hard for them to meet new people outside work.

154
00:10:39,379 --> 00:10:46,700
I feel very blessed to have had this thing in my life that like has connected me with so many amazing people like, you know, yourselves.

155
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:53,500
And friends that you'll have, regardless of what how your journey is within the music, if it stops or it falters in any way,

156
00:10:53,500 --> 00:10:57,580
connections that you'll have that last beyond that for the rest of your life.

157
00:10:57,580 --> 00:11:02,900
Now, you know, people, you know, I know Ben because of them, not because of the music, but because we're friends.

158
00:11:02,900 --> 00:11:05,300
Do you know what I mean? You gain so much from it.

159
00:11:05,300 --> 00:11:10,139
So, yeah, I'm glad you brought a community like that's something that is so important to us.

160
00:11:10,139 --> 00:11:12,259
We were talking about yesterday.

161
00:11:12,259 --> 00:11:19,340
It's one. It's one of the reasons we're really attracted to actually working with you guys was the fact that you were so heavily prioritizing

162
00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:26,820
the idea of community and how when we were getting into the scene, that was everything like it was all like, yeah,

163
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:29,460
you could recognize another raver from your outfit.

164
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:35,060
Yeah, it was oversized clothing and bucket hats and like, you know, whistles and stuff and glow sticks.

165
00:11:35,060 --> 00:11:37,260
It's like, oh, there's there's another raver.

166
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:40,340
And you instantly felt that rapport and that connection.

167
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:45,900
That's a little bit lost these days because I feel like everything's kind of generic and kind of washed out a little bit.

168
00:11:45,900 --> 00:11:50,300
And I think with social media and stuff, although obviously it connects everyone,

169
00:11:50,300 --> 00:11:56,700
it's also isolating in a way because people don't go out as much because they're getting dopamine hits and just sitting on their own in their bedroom.

170
00:11:56,740 --> 00:12:00,300
So in a way, we're kind of losing, although we're more connected,

171
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:03,820
there's a loss of community with all this tech and social media.

172
00:12:04,220 --> 00:12:12,620
And I think what you guys are doing with your WhatsApp group and all that kind of stuff is rejuvenating that idea and bringing everyone back together in a healthy way

173
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:14,820
and interacting with everyone.

174
00:12:14,820 --> 00:12:18,340
And when they come out to the nights, they'll recognize each other.

175
00:12:18,340 --> 00:12:19,780
Oh, I'm so and so from chat.

176
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:21,100
Oh, you're blah, blah, blah.

177
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:23,300
And then they had some virus at the weekend.

178
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:29,060
And they put faces to the names that they've been talking to for the last month or two months, whatever it might be.

179
00:12:29,060 --> 00:12:34,460
And I think that's really powerful and something that we both thought was really cool.

180
00:12:34,460 --> 00:12:36,340
Yeah, I'm super glad to hear that.

181
00:12:36,340 --> 00:12:38,620
Like, you know, we're figuring it out.

182
00:12:38,620 --> 00:12:43,580
You know, like you guys, I think you both talked about, I guess, in this increasingly digital world.

183
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:46,780
Like you can lose a bit of that connection and that warmth.

184
00:12:46,780 --> 00:12:53,260
And I don't know, like not everything's going to work, but we want to try because we felt like what it's done for us.

185
00:12:53,460 --> 00:12:58,260
And I still see people I meet in the scene who like those moments can still exist.

186
00:12:58,260 --> 00:13:01,940
But I feel they just may be a bit rarer, you know, and like they're even more special because of it.

187
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:05,620
Like I've got a friend I met, like through hospital events, I think.

188
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:07,700
And he met his his like raving crew.

189
00:13:07,700 --> 00:13:13,780
He was at a trade show for work and he had like a ram lanyard or something that he had put his pass in.

190
00:13:14,020 --> 00:13:16,620
And the person that was giving him a tour just like locked onto it.

191
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:20,420
Yeah, you know, like these little moments, they can still exist.

192
00:13:20,460 --> 00:13:23,300
But it feels like they can be a bit harder to find sometimes.

193
00:13:23,300 --> 00:13:28,940
You know, it's tribalistic coming together with a group of people listening to the same music.

194
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:30,100
It's it's very tribal.

195
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:36,340
And we're coming together as a as just one people under one roof to listen to this music.

196
00:13:36,340 --> 00:13:43,020
It's it's it's it's something to be it's a joyous thing is coming people coming together to enjoy that.

197
00:13:44,140 --> 00:13:50,380
Where like I say, we've we've lost that with everything's faceless and no contact and don't touch.

198
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:51,580
It's about touching.

199
00:13:51,580 --> 00:13:53,939
Yeah, it's about reaching out and touching the next person.

200
00:13:53,939 --> 00:13:54,539
Do you know what I'm saying?

201
00:13:54,539 --> 00:13:55,500
Yeah, slapping your mates back.

202
00:13:55,500 --> 00:14:01,460
When you know, I mean, it's those little micro things that make the difference with us as a people.

203
00:14:01,659 --> 00:14:05,379
Yeah, when we're told that that's bad and we shouldn't do this and we shouldn't connect,

204
00:14:05,860 --> 00:14:09,580
that's when shit starts, excuse my language, but that's when stuff goes starts going wrong.

205
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:12,779
Yeah, you know, when we're connecting as a people, things get fixed.

206
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:15,139
Yeah, I'm a firm believer of that.

207
00:14:15,139 --> 00:14:16,940
So you don't have to hold back on the podcast.

208
00:14:16,940 --> 00:14:18,340
You can say whatever you want.

209
00:14:18,340 --> 00:14:22,860
Yeah, I think connection is all we're creating, really.

210
00:14:22,980 --> 00:14:24,140
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

